.SS "Install"
.PP
.nf
$ npm install chalk
.fi
.PP
.SS "Usage"
.PP
.nf
const chalk = require('chalk');

console\&.log(chalk\&.blue('Hello world!'));
.fi
.PP

.PP
Chalk comes with an easy to use composable API where you just chain and nest the styles you want\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const chalk = require('chalk');
const log = console\&.log;

// Combine styled and normal strings
log(chalk\&.blue('Hello') + ' World' + chalk\&.red('!'));

// Compose multiple styles using the chainable API
log(chalk\&.blue\&.bgRed\&.bold('Hello world!'));

// Pass in multiple arguments
log(chalk\&.blue('Hello', 'World!', 'Foo', 'bar', 'biz', 'baz'));

// Nest styles
log(chalk\&.red('Hello', chalk\&.underline\&.bgBlue('world') + '!'));

// Nest styles of the same type even (color, underline, background)
log(chalk\&.green(
    'I am a green line ' +
    chalk\&.blue\&.underline\&.bold('with a blue substring') +
    ' that becomes green again!'
));

// ES2015 template literal
log(`
CPU: ${chalk\&.red('90%')}
RAM: ${chalk\&.green('40%')}
DISK: ${chalk\&.yellow('70%')}
`);

// ES2015 tagged template literal
log(chalk`
CPU: {red ${cpu\&.totalPercent}%}
RAM: {green ${ram\&.used / ram\&.total * 100}%}
DISK: {rgb(255,131,0) ${disk\&.used / disk\&.total * 100}%}
`);

// Use RGB colors in terminal emulators that support it\&.
log(chalk\&.keyword('orange')('Yay for orange colored text!'));
log(chalk\&.rgb(123, 45, 67)\&.underline('Underlined reddish color'));
log(chalk\&.hex('#DEADED')\&.bold('Bold gray!'));
.fi
.PP

.PP
Easily define your own themes:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const chalk = require('chalk');

const error = chalk\&.bold\&.red;
const warning = chalk\&.keyword('orange');

console\&.log(error('Error!'));
console\&.log(warning('Warning!'));
.fi
.PP

.PP
Take advantage of console\&.log \fRstring substitution\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const name = 'Sindre';
console\&.log(chalk\&.green('Hello %s'), name);
//=> 'Hello Sindre'
.fi
.PP
.SS "API"
.SS "chalk\&.\fR<style>[\&.<style>\&.\&.\&.](string, [string\&.\&.\&.])\fP"
Example: \fRchalk\&.red\&.bold\&.underline('Hello', 'world');\fP

.PP
Chain \fBstyles\fP and call the last one as a method with a string argument\&. Order doesn't matter, and later styles take precedent in case of a conflict\&. This simply means that \fRchalk\&.red\&.yellow\&.green\fP is equivalent to \fRchalk\&.green\fP\&.

.PP
Multiple arguments will be separated by space\&.
.SS "chalk\&.level"
Specifies the level of color support\&.

.PP
Color support is automatically detected, but you can override it by setting the \fRlevel\fP property\&. You should however only do this in your own code as it applies globally to all Chalk consumers\&.

.PP
If you need to change this in a reusable module, create a new instance:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const ctx = new chalk\&.Instance({level: 0});
.fi
.PP

.PP
Level  Description  \fR0\fP  All colors disabled  \fR1\fP  Basic color support (16 colors)  \fR2\fP  256 color support  \fR3\fP  Truecolor support (16 million colors)  
.SS "chalk\&.supportsColor"
Detect whether the terminal \fRsupports color\fP\&. Used internally and handled for you, but exposed for convenience\&.

.PP
Can be overridden by the user with the flags \fR--color\fP and \fR--no-color\fP\&. For situations where using \fR--color\fP is not possible, use the environment variable \fRFORCE_COLOR=1\fP (level 1), \fRFORCE_COLOR=2\fP (level 2), or \fRFORCE_COLOR=3\fP (level 3) to forcefully enable color, or \fRFORCE_COLOR=0\fP to forcefully disable\&. The use of \fRFORCE_COLOR\fP overrides all other color support checks\&.

.PP
Explicit 256/Truecolor mode can be enabled using the \fR--color=256\fP and \fR--color=16m\fP flags, respectively\&.
.SS "chalk\&.stderr and chalk\&.stderr\&.supportsColor"
\fRchalk\&.stderr\fP contains a separate instance configured with color support detected for \fRstderr\fP stream instead of \fRstdout\fP\&. Override rules from \fRchalk\&.supportsColor\fP apply to this too\&. \fRchalk\&.stderr\&.supportsColor\fP is exposed for convenience\&.
