.SS "Tagged template literal"
Chalk can be used as a \fRtagged template literal\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const chalk = require('chalk');

const miles = 18;
const calculateFeet = miles => miles * 5280;

console\&.log(chalk`
    There are {bold 5280 feet} in a mile\&.
    In {bold ${miles} miles}, there are {green\&.bold ${calculateFeet(miles)} feet}\&.
`);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Blocks are delimited by an opening curly brace (\fR{\fP), a style, some content, and a closing curly brace (\fR}\fP)\&.

.PP
Template styles are chained exactly like normal Chalk styles\&. The following three statements are equivalent:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(chalk\&.bold\&.rgb(10, 100, 200)('Hello!'));
console\&.log(chalk\&.bold\&.rgb(10, 100, 200)`Hello!`);
console\&.log(chalk`{bold\&.rgb(10,100,200) Hello!}`);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Note that function styles (\fRrgb()\fP, \fRhsl()\fP, \fRkeyword()\fP, etc\&.) may not contain spaces between parameters\&.

.PP
All interpolated values (\fR chalk`${foo}` \fP) are converted to strings via the \fR\&.toString()\fP method\&. All curly braces (\fR{\fP and \fR}\fP) in interpolated value strings are escaped\&.
.SS "256 and Truecolor color support"
Chalk supports 256 colors and \fRTruecolor\fP (16 million colors) on supported terminal apps\&.

.PP
Colors are downsampled from 16 million RGB values to an ANSI color format that is supported by the terminal emulator (or by specifying \fR{level: n}\fP as a Chalk option)\&. For example, Chalk configured to run at level 1 (basic color support) will downsample an RGB value of #FF0000 (red) to 31 (ANSI escape for red)\&.

.PP
Examples:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchalk\&.hex('#DEADED')\&.underline('Hello, world!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchalk\&.keyword('orange')('Some orange text')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchalk\&.rgb(15, 100, 204)\&.inverse('Hello!')\fP
.PP

.PP
Background versions of these models are prefixed with \fRbg\fP and the first level of the module capitalized (e\&.g\&. \fRkeyword\fP for foreground colors and \fRbgKeyword\fP for background colors)\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchalk\&.bgHex('#DEADED')\&.underline('Hello, world!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchalk\&.bgKeyword('orange')('Some orange text')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRchalk\&.bgRgb(15, 100, 204)\&.inverse('Hello!')\fP
.PP

.PP
The following color models can be used:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRrgb\fP\fP - Example: \fRchalk\&.rgb(255, 136, 0)\&.bold('Orange!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRhex\fP\fP - Example: \fRchalk\&.hex('#FF8800')\&.bold('Orange!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRkeyword\fP\fP (CSS keywords) - Example: \fRchalk\&.keyword('orange')\&.bold('Orange!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRhsl\fP\fP - Example: \fRchalk\&.hsl(32, 100, 50)\&.bold('Orange!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRhsv\fP\fP - Example: \fRchalk\&.hsv(32, 100, 100)\&.bold('Orange!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRhwb\fP\fP - Example: \fRchalk\&.hwb(32, 0, 50)\&.bold('Orange!')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRansi\fP\fP - Example: \fRchalk\&.ansi(31)\&.bgAnsi(93)('red on yellowBright')\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRansi256\fP\fP - Example: \fRchalk\&.bgAnsi256(194)('Honeydew, more or less')\fP
.PP
.SS "Windows"
If you're on Windows, do yourself a favor and use \fRWindows Terminal\fP instead of \fRcmd\&.exe\fP\&.
.SS "Origin story"
\fRcolors\&.js\fP used to be the most popular string styling module, but it has serious deficiencies like extending \fRString\&.prototype\fP which causes all kinds of \fRproblems\fP and the package is unmaintained\&. Although there are other packages, they either do too much or not enough\&. Chalk is a clean and focused alternative\&.
